+NOTES: str = """\
+Section 1.4 fixes the native group law (v,a)(w,b)=(v+a w,ab) and the
+conjugation formula.  Section 5, equation (CSE-12), fixes the exact constraints:
+all coefficients and the right-hand side are (v,1), while every z_i is either
+(zero,1) or (zero,2^{-1}).  The proof of Theorem 5.2 shows that a binary ISIS
+solution x corresponds exactly to z_i=(zero,(x_i+1)^{-1}).  The generator uses
+that correspondence in reverse; it never solves the generated equation.
+
+The certificate-producing algorithm on a public instance is therefore an
+algorithm for randomized binary ISIS.  Theorem 5.2 says that a PPT algorithm
+succeeding with non-negligible probability in n log(p)<m<p/(2n^4) yields a PPT
+algorithm for worst-case SIVP with approximation p n^6.  This is not the easy
+formula of Proposition 4.2: that proposition applies when some coefficient has
+nontrivial scalar component beta_i, whereas every coefficient here has beta_i=1.
+The unconstrained generic polynomial-time regime and the fixed-small-parameter
+enumeration regime are explicitly avoided.
+
+Inverse planting would not by itself inherit an average-case theorem.  Here it
+does up to a quantified statistical error: for distinct binary x,x', random A
+satisfies A x=A x' with probability p^{-n}, so A is a universal hash family.
+The leftover-hash bound for a uniform weight-m/2 source is
+0.5*sqrt(p^n/binomial(m,m/2)); the default m=Theta(n log n), p=Theta(m n^4)
+schedule makes it negligible.  Plants and coefficients have no per-position
+signature.  The panel tests a marginal score, greedy residual descent, balanced
+random restarts, and a construction-aware Kannan embedding followed by a sweep
+of complete floating-point LLL reductions.
+"""
